  "findings": [
    {
      "action": "GovernedOutcomeReference now carries occurred_at, taken from the outcome's own event or record; intake reads no clock. Every observation field is a function of the reference. The fail-closed rule is unchanged: same source identity with changed content is still refused.",
      "id": "W1-F1",
      "observed": "recorded_at was stamped from the intake clock onto a hash-bound observation, so the same governed outcome offered twice at different moments produced two content hashes and the second was refused as idempotency_key_reused.",
      "status": "fixed",
      "subject": "LearnedObservationIntake"
    },
    {
      "action": "CorpusFactoryRequest gained an optional split_group_key. When set, every item in the request takes the split derived from that key and the manifest records profile sprint21c3-group-aware-split-v1. Unset, behaviour is unchanged for every pre-C3 source.",
      "id": "W3-F1",
      "observed": "Splits were assigned by hashing each item's own lineage hash, so the five variants of one task and its correction examples could land in different splits. Section 4.12 requires all material for one task and one generator template to stay in one split.",
      "status": "fixed",
      "subject": "CorpusFactory split assignment"
    },
    {
      "action": "Generation takes a campaign epoch instead of a clock, and a resumed campaign passes back the bundle artifact its first run recorded. Measured: a resumed 30-task campaign skips 150 of 150 runs, starts zero containers, and finishes in 8 seconds instead of 156.",
      "id": "W3-F2",
      "observed": "RealityTaskManifest hashes its own created_at and its hidden-bundle artifact id. Both moved per run, so the same task produced a different manifest hash on every campaign - and the manifest hash is what binds an outcome to its task, what resume matches, and what the corpus plane deduplicates. Resume could therefore never skip anything.",
